WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.734
You know him

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:02.235
from his groundbreaking

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.037
blockbuster films,

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:06.339
Titanic, The Terminator,

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.108
the Avatar series.

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:10.243
His movies have won over 20 Oscars

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:12.679
and are some of the very highest grossing

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.881
and most expensive films of all time.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:16.249
But James

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:17.317
Cameron is much more

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.352
than an Academy Award winning filmmaker.

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.221
He's also a scientific

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.390
explorer, engineer,

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.625
environmentalist and pioneer

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.360
of new technologies.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.162
His innovations have descended

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:30.830
to the bottom of the ocean

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.999
and soared into space.

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.501
Cameron's latest documentary

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.070
series, Secrets of the Elephants,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.838
dives into the lives

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:41.274
of those majestic and mammoth animals.

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.643
It will premiere on April 20,

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.812
first on National Geographic.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:46.913
Cameron's commitment

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.014
to sustainability

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.383
also inspired a move to New Zealand,

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:51.751
where he and his wife

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.987
live on an organic farm.

00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:56.456
We sat down recently here in New York.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.658
James Cameron. What an honor.

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:00.026
It's a huge honor to be on

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.061
your show for it. Thank you.

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:04.297
So if you look at the four

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:05.632
highest grossing films

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.801
in the history of the world,

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.335
you've made three of them.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:11.371
Yeah, turns out that way.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.872
And what's extraordinary to me is,

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:13.807
I mean, each of these

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:16.342
has grossed more than $2 billion. Right.

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.079
But they do it all over the world.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:20.313
They do well everywhere.

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:24.551
I was in India when Avatar, the new one.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.286
The new one came out.

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:27.587
And India, as you

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.756
know, is consumed with Bollywood movies.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.658
Hollywood has very small presence

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:32.492
historically,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.561
except Avatar was everywhere.

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:35.261
Yeah.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.664
So my question to you is,

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:40.834
what is it that you have found

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:44.571
that appeals to so many people?

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:46.372
The things that I care about

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:49.042
are trying to reach people emotionally.

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:51.111
But I also am very visual.

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:51.578
Right.

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:52.312
And, you know,

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.813
I get a lot of ideas from dreams

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:54.547
and things like that.

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:55.448
And I think that taps

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:56.483
into the subconscious

00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:57.884
that we all kind of share,

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:59.919
if you believe that kind of young in

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.021
view of life.

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:03.323
But I also think about that

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.057
global market.

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:05.525
And I think I don't want to do stuff

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.560
that's just based on kind of

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.728
current mediums

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:10.864
and current little cultural,

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:13.199
you know, kind of ideas and trends.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:13.600
I want to

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.667
I want to go back to sort of

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:15.535
classic ideas,

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:16.936
almost back to Greek tragedy

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:17.270
and things

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.405
like that, things that affect everybody.

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:20.340
So, for example,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.442
the new Avatar film is about family

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.544
and bonds that we all

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:25.044
share in

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:25.712
any culture,

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:27.814
any religion, any language group

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:28.915
around the world.

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.750
Titanic, you know, about love

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.685
and sacrifice and duty,

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:34.020
which are things that,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:34.654
for example,

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:34.954
you know,

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.789
maybe in Asia

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.725
you know, mean a lot to people.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:39.692
So even though they had

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:40.760
a language barrier

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.896
to surmount, to watch the film,

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:44.964
it meant something to them.

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:45.131
You know,

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:45.999
so I try to just

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:48.601
tap into universals of human experience.

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:50.803
Beyond that,

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:52.172
I could get very specific

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.072
about visual effects

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.207
and things like that.

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.309
But I think it's that it's that universal

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.178
universal sense of who we are

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.946
as human beings.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:01.948
You said you think about the world

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.483
audience, a global audience. Yes.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:04.417
Do you think it helps

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:05.852
that you live in New Zealand?

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:06.352
You know, you've

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.621
got you sort of out of America?

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.123
I think it's interesting to live out.

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:13.092
I lived in in Los Angeles for 47 years.

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:15.228
I grew up in Canada before that.

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:16.796
Now that I live in New Zealand,

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:17.730
I think it's interesting

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.832
to look back on American culture

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:21.067
and society and politics

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.002
and so on from an outside viewpoint

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:24.070
and get a morning paper

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.439
that sees it in a very different way.

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:28.374
I think I think it's healthy,

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.443
healthy, and I think more Americans

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:31.811
should do that.

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:32.512
You know,

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:34.781
look into the bubble from the outside.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:35.381
I mean,

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:36.683
I think if you think about it

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:38.551
in a broader sense,

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.086
that's how the Avatar movies work.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:41.254
They ask us to see

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:42.522
through the eyes of nature,

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.157
through these blue people

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:46.025
back at ourselves.

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.027
And our sins against nature

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:49.028
that we collectively

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.097
and civilization doing.

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:53.266
I think maybe one in a thousand people

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.434
that goes to an Avatar movie

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:56.536
actually roots for the humans you know?

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:58.204
But we're a human audience

00:03:58.204 --> 00:03:59.906
rooting against ourselves.

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:01.341
If you think about it in a strange way,

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:02.742
through these kind

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.244
of aspirational characters

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:06.479
that really represent the best of us

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.281
or the way we imagine we can be

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:09.215
or maybe

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:09.549
the way

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.517
we used to be thousands of years ago.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:12.051
Yeah.

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:12.986
Do you think you know,

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:13.653
I think about this

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:15.388
because when you look at Asia,

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:17.190
which is modernizing and urbanizing

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:17.957
so fast

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:19.892
right there, in many ways

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.562
despoiling the environment even more.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.931
And yet the movies do very well in Asia.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:28.001
Yeah, I think we suffer collectively

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:30.303
from nature deficit disorder,

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:32.338
and we all need to reconnect.

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:32.672
And I think

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.040
because we were less urbanized

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.475
when we were kids, certainly my age

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:36.376
and maybe even the next

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:37.543
generation behind me,

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.814
we long for that sort of thing.

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:43.416
We had as children where we were

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:44.584
we were down in the grass,

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:45.652
we were down in the soil.

00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:47.620
We collected birds and butterflies

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.455
and butterflies and flowers

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:50.423
and things like that.

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:51.524
We don't get that anymore.

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:52.925
Our lives are so urbanized.

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:54.494
I mean, we're here in New York right now.

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:56.162
It's hard to even imagine

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:56.863
that, you know,

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:57.597
and you just look out

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.766
at that technical landscape.

00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:01.034
I think we long for it.

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:01.701
And so people

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:03.303
see something

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:05.405
that they that they want in their

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:06.339
in their dreams.

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:06.906
You know, there's

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:08.408
I think there's something

00:05:08.408 --> 00:05:11.311
that's very kind of primitive about it

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:13.880
and primitive in us, atavistic right.

00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:16.082
That we that we longed for.

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:16.649
And I just hope

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:17.850
we can reconnect with that

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.154
because, you know, we're our urban life

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:22.622
is devastating nature

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:24.257
in order to support it.

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:25.858
How does the subconscious work,

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:26.759
as best you can tell,

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:27.627
because you're saying

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:29.696
you get a lot of your ideas from dreams.

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.698
You're appealing to people

00:05:31.698 --> 00:05:32.265
in a way

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:33.700
that they don't think of themselves.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:34.600
I think a lot of people

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.669
as having this nature deficit disorder.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.171
I mean, they're much going around.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:38.604
Mm hmm.

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:40.206
But somehow, you know,

00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:41.307
it does feel like you're

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:43.543
tapping into that right?

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.144
Well, I do think I do

00:05:45.144 --> 00:05:46.045
think we have certain

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:47.280
innate programing

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.648
that's at a very deep level,

00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:50.616
much lower than higher brain function.

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:51.884
And I think our subconscious,

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.553
when it's operating

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:54.454
either in the background

00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:55.588
during the day or at night,

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:57.223
where it sort of takes over

00:05:57.223 --> 00:05:58.291
is sort of

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:00.226
its sort of processing internally

00:06:00.226 --> 00:06:01.661
in a way that scientists

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:02.495
don't really understand

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:04.931
dreams and the psychological purpose

00:06:04.931 --> 00:06:05.965
that they serve.

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:06.733
I think they're kind of

00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:09.035
like a generative a I you know,

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:10.970
I think they're making imagery

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:13.272
from a vast dataset.

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:15.208
That's our entire experience in life,

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:16.743
and it's just making imagery.

00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:17.944
And then another part of our brain

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:19.011
is supplying a narrative

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:19.746
that goes along with it.

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:20.313
And the narrative

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:22.682
doesn't always make much sense you know?

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:24.951
But we're storytelling to ourselves

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:26.552
all night, all night long.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.187
Or at least during REM sleep,

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.455
you know, and I'm not a dream actor.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:30.490
I don't want to say up front

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:31.357
or neuroscientists,

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:33.092
but I think this is what's happening.

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:34.761
And we're trying to make sense

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:35.361
of the world

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.697
because we don't really remember

00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:38.731
like a videotape.

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:40.633
We remember stories

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:41.901
like strings of beads.

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:43.536
You know, I went to a party, okay?

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:45.738
I remember where it was. That's a bead.

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:46.339
But you don't

00:06:46.339 --> 00:06:47.240
you can't playback

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.475
a whole tape of the whole thing.

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:52.078
I'll remember this as an image,

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:53.413
a picture of you

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:54.680
and some of the topics

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:55.415
that we talked about.

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:56.816
But I won't be able to play it back

00:06:56.816 --> 00:06:57.683
like a videotape.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.051
And if I do playback,

00:06:59.051 --> 00:06:59.419
you know,

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:00.186
a recording of it,

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:00.453
I'll go,

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:00.987
Oh, yeah,

00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:03.222
we said that that was cool or not

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:04.424
or embarrass myself.

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.192
I wish I could take it back.

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:06.626
So.

00:07:06.626 --> 00:07:07.126
So Billy

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:07.960
Joel said to me

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.730
that he gets a lot of his his music

00:07:11.564 --> 00:07:13.766
from from dreams and that,

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:14.400
you know,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.168
he he'd get up

00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:17.103
and or even later on, he realized

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:18.037
it's always

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:19.772
the core, which was for him,

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.374
always the melody,

00:07:21.374 --> 00:07:22.775
the harmony, not the words.

00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:24.811
The words were later added. Interesting.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:27.413
What do you explain how your dreams work?

00:07:27.413 --> 00:07:28.214
Sounds to me.

00:07:28.214 --> 00:07:29.449
Like for him, it's musical.

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:31.684
For me, it's images of images.

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:33.553
And, you know, so

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:34.687
so just a bunch of random,

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.122
sometimes very surreal images,

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:37.023
sometimes beautiful,

00:07:37.023 --> 00:07:38.591
sometimes terrifying.

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:39.892
And I

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:41.127
lately been thinking that there's

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:42.161
a little bit of it's

00:07:42.161 --> 00:07:43.563
not just the picture.

00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:44.831
There's a little metadata

00:07:44.831 --> 00:07:45.865
running underneath that

00:07:45.865 --> 00:07:47.867
that's telling me what the picture means.

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:48.601
You know,

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:49.335
so, for example,

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:50.369
the Terminator was based

00:07:50.369 --> 00:07:51.838
on a single image

00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:53.072
of a chrome skeleton

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:54.740
emerging out of fire.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.042
But the metadata said

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:56.642
it used

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.678
to look like a man covered in flesh

00:07:58.678 --> 00:08:00.613
and the fire burned it away.

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:01.814
And so it's it's

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:03.483
actually a story segment.

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:04.350
And then you start putting

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.685
those story segments together

00:08:05.685 --> 00:08:07.453
and then starts to turn it into a story

00:08:07.453 --> 00:08:09.355
you want to tell to other people.

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:10.790
And I think storytellers

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.091
sitting around the campfire

00:08:12.091 --> 00:08:13.893
in a cave 50,000 years ago

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:15.294
were doing the same thing.

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:17.263
And so when you get those images,

00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:18.831
do you say to yourself,

00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:19.365
you know,

00:08:19.365 --> 00:08:20.800
I put I'll just mechanically do

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:21.534
write it down.

